# Line endings.
#
# The repository had no rule, and core.autocrlf was off, so whatever
# endings a file happened to be written with were what got committed:
# 603 files stored LF, 155 stored CRLF, and five were mixed within a
# single file. Any tool that rewrote a file whole then flipped its
# endings and showed every line as changed, which buries a one-line
# edit in a thousand-line diff and conflicts with every other branch
# that touches the file.
#
# Git now stores LF for all text, and checks LF back out on every
# platform, so neither a tool nor an editor can reintroduce CRLF.

# The default: detect text, store it with LF.
* text=auto

# Declared rather than detected.
#
# Git guesses whether a file is text, and it guessed wrong at least once
# here: daily-schedule/index.tsx was classified binary (it is valid UTF-8
# with no NUL bytes; the mixed endings appear to have confused the
# heuristic), so git would neither diff nor merge it. Naming the
# extensions settles it.
*.ts            text eol=lf
*.tsx           text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.mdc           text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.svg           text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.webmanifest   text eol=lf
*.log           text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.cursorrules    text eol=lf
*.example       text eol=lf

# PowerShell keeps CRLF: it is a Windows script, and the 5.1 parser is
# particular about line endings inside here-strings.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.stackdump binary

# Generated; a diff of it is noise either way.
*.lock  text eol=lf -diff
